# Technical Trust Canon (digest)

Full canon: docs/CANON.md — read it before any content, naming, data-model, or copy change. The canon wins over this digest if they ever disagree.

## Three structures. They NEVER blur.
- The Four C's (Competence, Comprehension, Candor, Consistency) are the PERCEPTION model — what a BUYER checks for across a relationship. They are NOT rows on the map.
- The map rows (Honesty, Understanding, Clarity, Judgment) are the PRODUCTION model — what a PRACTITIONER does in a moment. Map columns (Discovery, Demo, Docs, Support) are interaction SURFACES, not phases — never a sequence, funnel, or timeline.
- The Trust Lifecycle (discovery → architecture → adoption) is the TIME dimension — when verdicts accumulate. Stages begin and end; surfaces recur across stages.
- Bridge (all three): "Buyers are checking for competence, comprehension, candor, and consistency. The map is where the checks happen. The lifecycle is when the verdicts accumulate."
- Shorter bridge (lifecycle not in play): "Buyers are checking for competence, comprehension, candor, and consistency. The map is where the checks happen."
- Competence is the precondition the map assumes. Never place it on the grid.
- Consistency has no surface — it is a *record* of behavior, not a behavior. Three pillar checks (competence, comprehension, candor) can be failed at a surface; Consistency can only be read off the clock. Never place Consistency on the grid.
- Asymmetry law: trust is accumulated on the clock and forfeited on the map.

## Register: "Discovery" vs "the discovery stage"
- "Discovery" (capitalized, standalone) = the map surface (a conversation type).
- "The discovery stage" (lowercase, always with the word *stage*) = the lifecycle period.
- Do not rename either. Do not treat density as confinement.

## Names are canonical.
- The 16 Pattern/Practice pairs are defined in docs/CANON.md §5. `lib/patterns.ts` must match the canon exactly.
- Never invent, rename, or "improve" a Pattern or Practice name in passing. A rename is an AMENDMENT: update docs/CANON.md (bump version, add amendment-log row), update patterns.ts, add a framework changelog entry — all in the same change.
- Naming laws: Patterns are "The ___", concrete and recognizable from the inside. Practices are executable MOVES, never virtues. Pairs read as mirrors. Max 2–3 announced Patterns at any time.

## Data laws.
- `lib/patterns.ts` is the single source of truth for Pattern/Practice data; `lib/framework.ts` for grid structure. Never duplicate this data in components, pages, or copy.
- Kit may hydrate publish fields (status, readUrl, date) by matching edition titles to existing Pattern names — it must never invent names or cells. See `lib/pattern-hydration.ts`.
- Pages render from data, never hand-written content lists (the homepage staleness rule).
- Shareable state lives in URLs, never localStorage.
- Canonical edition domain: newsletter.technicaltrust.org.

## Voice.
- Framing copy uses manifesto cadence: short declarative lines, one idea per line.
- Never motivational-poster. Practices are moves. Teach before persuading.
- Brand compression: "Flip it."

## The canon is internal.
- Never import docs/CANON.md into app code, never route to it, never link it from public pages, never quote it as a citable document in site copy. Its IDEAS ship; the document does not.

## When a request conflicts with the canon:
Flag the conflict and propose it as an amendment instead of silently complying or silently refusing.
